The same ticket shape, declared three ways.

```csharp
// A positional record: immutable, value equality, deconstruction, with-expressions.
public record TicketDto(string Id, string Subject, TicketStatus Status);

var a = new TicketDto("t-1", "Printer jam", TicketStatus.Open);
var b = new TicketDto("t-1", "Printer jam", TicketStatus.Open);
bool same = a == b;                                  // true — every member matches

var closed = a with { Status = TicketStatus.Closed }; // new object; a is unchanged
var (id, subject, _) = a;                             // deconstruction

// A class entity: identity is the Id, not the field values.
public class Ticket
{
    public required string Id { get; init; }
    public string Subject { get; set; } = "";
    public TicketStatus Status { get; set; }
}

var t1 = new Ticket { Id = "t-1", Subject = "Printer jam" };
var t2 = new Ticket { Id = "t-1", Subject = "Printer jam" };
bool alsoSame = t1 == t2;                            // false — reference comparison

// An immutable class: mutability is a separate axis from equality.
public class AuditStamp
{
    public required string Actor { get; init; }
    public required DateTimeOffset At { get; init; }
}
```

The entity is the interesting case. `t1` and `t2` describe the same ticket, and yet `==` is false — which is correct, because a `Ticket` whose subject is edited is still that ticket. Value equality would say the edit produced a different ticket, and any code that relied on it would misbehave the moment a field changed.

