A calculator that needs the current time, written twice.

```csharp
// Inheritance: couples the calculator to a base class's data and construction.
public class ClockBase { protected DateTimeOffset Now() => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow; }

public class SlaCalculatorInherited : ClockBase
{
    public bool IsBreached(Ticket t) => Now() - t.OpenedAt > TimeSpan.FromHours(4);
}
// Testing this means subclassing it, and the calculator can never inherit anything else.

// Composition: couples the calculator to one small promise.
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset Now { get; } }

public class SystemClock : IClock { public DateTimeOffset Now => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow; }

public class SlaCalculator
{
    private readonly IClock _clock;
    public SlaCalculator(IClock clock) => _clock = clock;

    public bool IsBreached(Ticket t) => _clock.Now - t.OpenedAt > TimeSpan.FromHours(4);
}

// A test supplies a fixed clock — no subclassing, no waiting four hours.
public sealed class FixedClock : IClock
{
    public FixedClock(DateTimeOffset now) => Now = now;
    public DateTimeOffset Now { get; }
}
```

The composed version is testable because the seam is explicit and narrow: `IClock` promises exactly one thing. Note also that the inherited version cannot inherit anything else, since C# allows only one base class — a cost you pay immediately and permanently in exchange for saving one constructor parameter.

