A generic repository, and the constraint that makes it work.

```csharp
public interface IEntity { string Id { get; } }

public class Repository<T> where T : class, IEntity
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, T> _byId = new();

    public void Add(T item) => _byId[item.Id] = item;   // legal because of : IEntity
    public T? Find(string id) => _byId.TryGetValue(id, out var found) ? found : null;
}
```

Without `where T : IEntity` the compiler rejects `item.Id`, because an unconstrained `T` might be any type at all. Without `where T : class` the `T?` return would mean a nullable value type, which is a different thing again.

And the parameter-type decision, with the trap:

```csharp
public int CountBreached(IEnumerable<Ticket> tickets)
{
    var total = tickets.Count();          // walks the sequence
    var breached = tickets.Count(t => t.IsBreached);  // walks it a SECOND time
    return breached;
}
```

If the caller passes a `List<Ticket>` this is merely wasteful. If it passes a lazily evaluated query — the next chapter's subject — the entire query runs twice, which may mean two round trips to the database. Accepting `IReadOnlyList<Ticket>` would state the requirement honestly, and materialising once with `ToList()` is the fix when the parameter type cannot change.

